\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Joint-embedding predictive architectures (JEPAs) learn a representation by predicting, in embedding space, the representation of a target part of an input from that of a context part~\citep{lecun2022path,assran2023ijepa,bardes2024vjepa}.
They use no negatives and, unlike masked autoencoders~\citep{he2022mae}, reconstruct no pixels, but the objective admits a trivial minimiser, a constant encoder, and the representation can also collapse \emph{dimensionally}, spanning only a few directions~\citep{jing2022dimensional}, which degrades downstream performance~\citep{garrido2023rankme}.
JEPAs and their non-contrastive relatives~\citep{grill2020byol,chen2021simsiam,baevski2022data2vec,caron2021dino} avoid collapse through design choices: predictor depth, width and learning rate, masking ratio and geometry, a stop-gradient with an exponential moving average (EMA) target.
Several have partial theory~\citep{tian2021understanding,richemond2023edge,halvagal2023implicit,littwin2024jepa}, but no criterion predicts \emph{when} a configuration collapses and how much rank it keeps.

We build such an account on one premise: collapse is decided early, near the collapsed solution, where a \emph{driving force}, the predictive signal shared by context and target, grows each direction of representation space and a \emph{decay}, from weight decay and from the self-prediction pressure of the loss, shrinks it.
Recent work develops this picture as a stability count at initialisation with the predictor held fixed~\citep{demelocosta2026drive}.
Once the predictor is also decayed this count does not decide survival: the collapsed solution is locally stable and the predictor's gain decays, so survival is decided by a race between a direction's transient growth and the decay of the predictor.
Analyses of linear BYOL/SimSiam and of optimal predictors give a static threshold in weight decay~\citep{tian2021understanding,wang2021directset,ponce2025dual,arbel2026peira}, with the predictor as small as the encoder; at a standard initialisation the predictor is of order one, the drive is first order, and survival depends on how far a direction grows before the predictor decays. Our contributions:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
\item \textbf{The race criterion} (\cref{def:race}): in the unbalanced regime of a standard initialisation, when a direction captures the decaying gain before the gain leaves its growth window; it has no fitted constants and predicts a critical weight decay $\lambda_k^{\mathrm{race}}\approx h_k(p_0)/\log(p_0/a_0)$ far below the static threshold, a rank non-monotone in weight decay, the need for predictor weight decay, and how predictor learning rate, depth and target speed act (\cref{sec:heuristics}).
\item \textbf{Tests} (\cref{sec:experiments}). On \EoneBRuns{} held-out, pre-registered linear-JEPA runs the race predicts the rank with error \EoneBAlignedMae{}, against \EoneBAlignedWindowMae{} for the stability count at initialisation (exceeded as a bound in \DvdAlignedOver{} of runs) and \EoneBAlignedBoundMae{} for the static threshold, and under masking \EtwoCycMae{} (static: \EtwoCycBoundMae{}); it fails for deep predictors (corrected post hoc and re-tested), one EMA sign and a tanh encoder under random masks. In a ViT a step-0 rule orders an SGD arm by weight decay but misplaces the transition.
\item \textbf{Beyond gradient descent, depth and the near-collapse window} (\cref{sec:ext}). Under AdamW in an aligned basis the count settles at $\#\{k:c_k/\sigma_k>\theta\}$, independent of weight decay, and a frozen phase model predicts it at a fixed horizon (error \ExtAtwopMae{}; static bound \ExtAtwopStaticMae{}) but not at convergence. With a deep linear encoder survival is a power law in the initial scale \eqref{eq:deep}, predicted with aligned factors only. A post-capture model of weight-decay schedules fails on short pulses. In a ViT raising weight decay removes directions as late as epoch 20 of 50: the premise fails.
\end{itemize}
